<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.17.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02054</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.17.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>6ad5aff4-a7bd-485b-98b6-5feff1a05507</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="8182e4b5-6834-4eff-ba26-a3344aa0818c" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--1-->
  <exercise data-content-id="2252dcce-e1e0-4a08-b5c2-468676440321" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="2" data-solution="No" data-solution-options="[&quot;Yes&quot;, &quot;No&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Here is the graph of quadratic function \(f\).</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f6d2f05756f240bf0fbb2729b1726aedba9ccbb7"/></media></figure></para>
      <para>Andre uses the expression \((x−5)^2+7\) to define \(f\).</para>
      <para>Noah uses the expression \((x+5)^2−7\) to define \(f\).</para>
      <para>Do you agree with either of them? </para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para> Correct! No. The vertex of the graph is (5,-7). The graph of Andre’s function would have a vertex at (5,7), and the graph of Noah’s function would have a vertex at (-5,-7).</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.17.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--2a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="282d6d0c-46f8-46a0-badc-fc34c38a8ccd" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="2" data-solution="The shape is the same but they are in different locations." data-solution-options="[&quot;They all have the same vertex.&quot;, &quot;The shape is the same but they are in different locations.&quot;, &quot;They all have a negative a coefficient in the vertex form of the equation. &quot;, &quot;All three graphs contain the point \\((0,0)\\).&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Here are the graphs of \(f(x)=x^2\), \(f(x)=x^2−5\), and \(f(x)=(x+2)^2−8\).</para>
      <para> <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b28e72de3d5826c42cdf5b308f3eff1a1537d56c"/></media></figure></para>
      <para> How do the three graphs compare?</para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The shape is the same but they are in different locations.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.17.2.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--2b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="44662707-e10a-4387-b777-c39c27a3d344" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="2" data-solution="Subtracting 5 from the squared term shifts the graph down by 5 units." data-solution-options="[&quot;Subtracting 5 from the squared term shifts the graph left by 5 units.&quot;, &quot;Subtracting 5 from the squared term shifts the graph right by 5 units.&quot;, &quot;Subtracting 5 from the squared term shifts the graph up by 5 units.&quot;, &quot;Subtracting 5 from the squared term shifts the graph down by 5 units.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> Compare the graphs of \(f(x)=x^2\) and \(f(x)=x^2−5\). What role does the -5 play in the comparison?</para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! Subtracting 5 from the squared term shifts the graph down by 5 units.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.17.2.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--2c-->
  <exercise data-content-id="72dc86cb-6e3f-4c54-8a83-e694249dc1cd" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="2" data-solution="Adding 2 to \(x\) before squaring shifts the graph of \(f(x)=x^2\) to the left by 2 units and subtracting 8 from the squared term shifts the graph down by 8 units." data-solution-options="[&quot;Adding 2 to \\(x\\) before squaring shifts the graph of \\(f(x)=x^2\\) to the left by 2 units and subtracting 8 from the squared term shifts the graph down by 8 units.&quot;, &quot;Adding 2 to \\(x\\) before squaring shifts the graph of \\(f(x)=x^2\\) to the up by 2 units and subtracting 8 from the squared term shifts the graph left by 8 units.&quot;, &quot;Adding 2 to \\(x\\) before squaring shifts the graph of \\(f(x)=x^2\\) to the right by 2 units and subtracting 8 from the squared term shifts the graph up by 8 units.&quot;, &quot;Adding 2 to \\(x\\) before squaring shifts the graph of \\(f(x)=x^2\\) to the down by 2 units and subtracting 8 from the squared term shifts the graph right by 8 units.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> Compare the graphs of \(f(x)=x^2\) and \(f(x)=(x+2)^2−8\). What roles do the \(+2\) and \(-8\) play in the comparison?</para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! Adding 2 to \(x\) before squaring shifts the graph of \(f(x)=x^2\) to the left by 2 units and subtracting 8 from the squared term shifts the graph down by 8 units.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.17.2.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="1f55aa1e-b16c-4b06-a84d-cc7775bce93f" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-retry-limit="2" data-solution="[&quot;\\(f(x)=(x−3)^2+2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=-(x−4)^2+3\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=-2(x−3)^2+1\\)&quot;]" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(f(x)=x^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=(x−1)^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=(x−3)^2+2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=2(x−4)^2−5\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=0.5(x+2)^2+6\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=-(x−4)^2+3\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=-2(x−3)^2+1\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para><emphasis>Select the three</emphasis> equations with a graph whose vertex has both a positive \(x\) and a positive \(y\).</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.17.2 – 7.17.5. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--4-->
  <exercise data-content-id="7667ebf6-4a0e-43ee-ad68-e4122e29fcd1" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="2" data-solution="\(f(x)=-(x+7)^2+3\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(f(x)=-(x+7)^2+3\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=-(x-7)^2+3\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=(x+7)^2+3\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=(x-7)^2+3\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Which equation represents the graph shown?<figure><media alt="GRAPH OF A DOWNWARD PARABOLA WITH A VERTEX AT (NEGATIVE 7, 3"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/e4e10406bde154391ac0790d52a32789409c6a48"/></media></figure></para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para> Correct! \(f(x)=-(x+7)^2+3\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.17.5.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--5-->
  <exercise data-content-id="9a934902-5287-41e8-9605-420df0d5afb2" data-problem-comparator="math" data-problem-type="input" data-retry-limit="2" data-solution="f(x)=-(x+4)^{2}-16">
    <problem>
      <para>The graph representing \(f(x)=x^2\) is shifted 4 units to the left, 16 units down, and flipped so it opens downward (reflects over the \(x\)-axis). Which equation defines the curve?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(f(x)=-(x+4)^2-16\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.17.5. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--6-->
  <exercise data-content-id="98eb9153-ec2e-4bec-8e61-67ae884326f0" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="2" data-solution="\(f(x) = (\frac12x)^2\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(f(x) =\\frac 12 x^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x) = x^2 + \\frac12\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x) = (x-\\frac 12)^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x) = (\\frac12x)^2\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>The graph of \(f(x) = x^2\) is transformed by a horizontal dilation of \(\frac12\). What is the equation of the function that results from this translation? </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para> Correct! \(f(x) = (\frac12x)^2\) </para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.17.5.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--7-->
  <exercise data-content-id="e7ef5d3c-1203-48a7-bcbc-cc6e2525319c" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-retry-limit="2" data-solution="[&quot;A horizontal dilation by a factor of 2&quot;, &quot;A vertical shift down 5 units&quot;]" data-solution-options="[&quot;A horizontal shift 2 units to the right&quot;, &quot;A horizontal dilation by a factor of 2&quot;, &quot;A horizontal shift left 5 units&quot;, &quot;A vertical shift down 5 units&quot;, &quot;A vertical dilation by a factor of 2&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>A function \(g(x) = (2x)^2 - 5\) was transformed from the parent quadratic function. Which of the following answer choices describes the transformations that were applied? </para>
      <para>Select the<emphasis> two</emphasis> descriptions that apply.</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! A horizontal dilation by a factor of 2 and A vertical shift down 5 units </para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.17.2 – 7.17.5. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--8-->
  <exercise data-content-id="0a126082-3fce-4bb1-afd0-2bbcb81e7ef9" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="2" data-solution="\(f(x) = (\frac 52x)^2\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(f(x) = (-1x)^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x) = (\\frac 15x)^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x) = (\\frac 52x)^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x) = x^2\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Which of the following represents a function that has experienced a horizontal compression? </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para> Correct! \(f(x) = (\frac 52x)^2\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.17.5.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Do not edit below this line-->
  <solution> </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response"> </div>
</div></section>
</content>
